{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789026789,"nonce":"nRUO6DqRazsgI+m2uUrgaWgb+z8=","title":"Derive the vector lane count from the element width","message":"`VF` is a constant 4 in widen.rs whatever the element is, so only a 4-byte type\nproduces a full 128-bit vector. A byte element gives `i8x4` (32 bits) and a\nshort gives `i16x4` (64) -- widths no aarch64 rule names, which reach the\nbackends as an ISLE \"no rule matched for term vector_size\" and as \"VecSplat\ndestination is not a vector\". 0b6dd62 refuses those rather than emitting them,\nwhich is correct but gives up the optimisation for every narrow element.\n\nrayzor's Cranelift backend already has the mapping this wants\n(`compiler/src/codegen/cranelift_backend.rs`):\n\n    (F32, 4) -\u003e F32X4     (I8|U8, 16) -\u003e I8X16     (I32|U32, 4) -\u003e I32X4\n    (F64, 2) -\u003e F64X2     (I16|U16, 8) -\u003e I16X8    (I64|U64, 2) -\u003e I64X2\n\nEvery one is 128 bits, and the lane count falls out of the element width:\n`VF = MACHINE_VECTOR_BYTES / bytes`.\n\nTwo things make this more than a substitution in ash:\n\n* **VF has to become per-loop, not per-value.** rayzor types each value on its\n  own because it lowers explicit SIMD ops; a loop widener cannot, because lane\n  i of one vector must be lane i of every other. So one VF per loop, computed\n  from the element width.\n* **A loop of mixed widths has no legal VF.** With one VF, element `e` yields\n  `e * VF` bytes; keeping the widest inside 128 bits forces `VF = 16 / max_e`,\n  and then every narrower element underfills again. Such a loop has to be\n  refused (or split, which is more machinery than this needs).\n\nThe 12 use sites are `t % VF`, the epilogue test and its `\u0026 ~(VF-1)` mask, the\nfive `new_vector_value(ty, reg, VF)` calls, `step * VF` in retime_induction,\nand lanes_fit itself. Each takes the loop's VF instead of the constant.\n\nWorth doing: it is what makes byte and short loops -- string and Bytes work,\nwhere vectorising pays most -- widenable at all.","files":null}]}